 entirely of \u03b1-ketoacids (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41557-020-00560-7)\n  20. Formation of urea and guanidine by irradiation of ammonium cyanide (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01660246)\n  21. Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a (https://europepmc.org/api/getPdf?pmcid=PMC4568310)\n  22. Cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanosulfidic_prebiotic_synthesis)\n  23. Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.07725)\n  24. Guanidine synthesis by guanylation (https://www.organic-chemistry.org/synthesis/C1N/guanidines.shtm)\n  25. Extraterrestrial amino acids and L\u2010enantiomeric excesses in the \\<fc\\>CM\\</fc\\>2 carbonaceous chondrites Aguas Zarcas and Murchison - Glavin - 2021 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/maps.13451)\n  26. The stereochemistry of amino acids in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0301926800001236)\n  27. Purines and triazines in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0016703775901015)\n  28. Extraterrestrial nucleobases in the Murchison meteorite - ScienceDirect (https://sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012821X08001866?via=ihub%3D)\n  29. (PDF) The Hydrolysis of Arginine (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/9309353_The_Hydrolysis_of_Arginine)\n  30. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein ... (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=167961801406af19852f76f62e796fa905c99643&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\n  31. Reasons for the occurrence of the twenty coded protein amino acids - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1981JMolE..17..273W)\n  32. Consensus temporal order of amino acids and evolution of the triplet code (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12166018_Consensus_temporal_order_of_amino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine, with no assumed enantioselectivity\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct a target-directed hypothesis network from HCN, acetylene, ammonia and cyanamide. The supplied literature does not establish a complete prebiotic synthesis of free arginine. Accordingly, the ten pathways below are explicitly conditional variants: five possible timings of side-chain guanidinylation, each paired with either sulfide-driven or sulfite/ferrocyanide-driven photochemistry. They are not ten independently demonstrated syntheses. Early guanidinylation minimizes competition from the eventual alpha-amino group; later guanidinylation postpones exposure of the guanidino group to hydrolysis conditions. The crucial carbon-chain-extension edges are explicitly labeled unresolved, lumped transformations rather than established elementary reactions. Their atom balance does not establish their chemical feasibility. Numerical condition ranges are proposed screening windows, not verified reproductions of published protocols. All chemistry is anoxic and assigned to surface settings; no unsupported hydrothermal transfer is introduced. Chiral products have unspecified stereochemistry and would generally be racemic. Protonation equilibria, transient species and competing-product networks are not separately enumerated. Sulfate and elemental sulfur are bookkeeping coproducts labeled intermediate because the required role vocabulary lacks a byproduct category. The network exceeds the requested minimum size to represent chemically distinct intermediates without duplicating molecule nodes.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"SO3^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Sulfite\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Sulfite ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"SO4^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Sulfate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Sulfate ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-Aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-(Carbamimidamido)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Beta-guanidinopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-Amino-2-hydroxypentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ornithine-precursor cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12N4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(Carbamimidamido)-2-hydroxypentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine-precursor cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-Diaminopentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ornithine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H13N5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H13N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-Diaminopentanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ornithinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H15N5O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Argininamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-Diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H12N2O2/c6-3-1-2-4(7)5(8)9/h4H,1-3,6-7H2,(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-mediated hydrocyanation of acetylene\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I) complexes\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I) complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic gas-liquid contact in a locally copper-bearing pool; proposed temperature window. Cyanide and sulfide speciation must not remove all catalytically available copper.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-mediated addition of hydrogen cyanide across the acetylene triple bond produces acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This supplies the experimentally supported C3 entry scaffold without assuming a pre-existing amino-acid skeleton. Acetylene-to-acrylonitrile chemistry is included in Patel et al. (2015). Copper availability, cyanide complexation and sulfide precipitation constrain environmental implementation; the supplied report does not establish the exact operating window.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-bearing, mildly alkaline solution; avoid prolonged exposure that favors additional addition or oligomerization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aza-Michael addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile followed by proton transfer forms beta-aminopropionitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) identify this conversion as the entry to the relevant amino-nitrile branch. It introduces the nitrogen that could eventually become arginine's side-chain guanidino linkage. Formation of this precursor is supported; conversion of the integrated mixture to free arginine is not.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated aqueous cyanamide and aminonitrile; pH screened within approximately 8-10. No rare-metal laboratory catalyst is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal primary amine adds to cyanamide's nitrile carbon, followed by proton transfer and guanidine tautomerization.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This substrate-specific guanidinylation is reported as relatively inefficient by Patel et al. (2015). Its chemical advantage is that there is not yet a competing alpha-amino group. Residual cyanamide, unreacted aminonitrile and cyanamide self-reaction products must be expected; no high conversion is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Unresolved sulfide-driven homologation of the guanidinylated C3 scaffold\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"Phosphate buffer\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic UV-irradiated, near-neutral solution with staged reagent delivery. This is a proposed multistage conversion, not a one-pot protocol. S8 represents a formal zero-valent sulfur endpoint.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped hypothesis requiring nitrile reduction, carbon-chain extension with net deoxygenation through sulfur-containing intermediates, and terminal cyanohydrin formation. Hydrogen sulfide supplies reducing equivalents; ammonia records nitrogen lost during nitrile-to-carbonyl conversions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE CONNECTING EDGE. Patel et al. (2015) support related cyanosulfidic intermediates and a downstream arginine aminonitrile, but the supplied findings do not demonstrate this complete conversion from the isolated guanidinylated nitrile. In particular, ordinary cyanohydrin homologation alone would introduce unwanted hydroxylation; a successful deoxygenating sequence is indispensable and unresolved here. The efficient aldehyde-cyanamide adduct chemistry in that paper also prevents assuming a simple uninterrupted open-chain route. Sulfide availability is an additional constraint (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Unresolved sulfite-driven homologation of the guanidinylated C3 scaffold\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferrocyanide photoredox mediator\",\n        \"Phosphate buffer\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ferrocyanide/ferricyanide photoredox couple, with turnover assumed but unverified for this substrate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic UV exposure in a sulfite-bearing pool; near-neutral pH. Sulfate is a formal oxidation endpoint, not an asserted exclusive sulfur product.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed photoreductive chain extension and cyanohydrin formation using sulfite oxidation to supply reducing equivalents through an iron-cyanide mediator.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE SUBSTRATE AND MECHANISM TRANSFER. Xu et al. (2018) demonstrate the utility of sulfite plus ferrocyanide for related photoreductive chemistry, not this arginine branch. Replacing a photoreductant does not automatically replace sulfide's separate role as a sulfur feedstock for thioamide chemistry. Consequently, this edge additionally requires discovery of a compatible deoxygenation mechanism and is less supported than rxn_004. It is retained as a conditional alternative because local sulfite and sulfide availability can differ substantially (Ranjan et al., 2018), not as an established substitute.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Unresolved sulfide-driven construction of the ornithine-precursor cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"Phosphate buffer\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, staged UV photochemistry near neutral pH. Open-chain aminoaldehyde capture would have to outcompete intramolecular cyclization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lumped hypothesis for reductive and deoxygenative extension of beta-aminopropionitrile followed by cyanohydrin capture of a transient four-carbon aminoaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE SKELETON-CONSTRUCTION BOTTLENECK. Related aminonitrile photoreduction is supported by Patel et al. (2015), but the report does not establish this free-amine homologation sequence. A transient 4-aminobutanal can cyclize to a five-membered imine, competing strongly with the desired open-chain chemistry. Ornithine detection in spark-discharge samples does not validate this mechanism (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011). This edge is necessary only for testing the delayed-guanidinylation variants represented here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Unresolved sulfite-driven construction of the ornithine-precursor cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferrocyanide photoredox mediator\",\n        \"Phosphate buffer\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ferrocyanide/ferricyanide photoredox couple, with turnover assumed but unverified for this substrate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic UV irradiation in a near-neutral sulfite-bearing pool. Neither free-amine compatibility nor the required deoxygenation has been established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed reductive chain extension using sulfite-derived reducing equivalents, followed by formation of the open-chain amino cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE CONNECTING EDGE with two independent gaps: the unprotected aminoaldehyde can cyclize, and a sulfite/ferrocyanide system has not been shown to reproduce the sulfur-dependent chain-extension chemistry. Xu et al. (2018) provide only electron-generation precedent. This is an explicit experimental hypothesis motivated by alternative local reductant availability, not a demonstrated ornithine synthesis (Ranjan et al., 2018; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidinylation before introduction of the alpha-amino group\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH screening near 8-10; no selective yield assumed. Cyanohydrin dissociation, aldehyde capture and cyanamide self-reaction must be monitored.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amine adds to cyanamide to create a guanidino group while the alpha position remains a cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE SUBSTRATE EXTENSION of amine-cyanamide guanidinylation. Its chemical merit is the absence of a competing alpha-amino group, unlike guanidinylation of ornithine or its aminonitrile. Patel et al. (2015) establish related amine guanidinylation but also efficient competing cyanamide-aldehyde chemistry. No experiment in the supplied report demonstrates this particular cyanohydrin conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker conversion of the guanidinylated cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-bearing solution with reversible cyanohydrin chemistry. The stated range is proposed; original experimental conditions require primary-source verification.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin equilibration releases an aldehyde and cyanide; ammonia-derived imine formation followed by cyanide addition gives the alpha-aminonitrile. This is not assumed to be direct displacement of hydroxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied report attributes a 90% conversion of cyanohydrin 45 to arginine alpha-aminonitrile 46 to Patel et al. (2015), while noting that the integrated network outcome was conditional. This edge represents that reported reaction class using a neutral open-chain constitutional representation; the cited yield is not assigned to upstream assembly or to arginine production. Free arginine was not reported.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker conversion of the free-amine cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-bearing aqueous solution; proposed pH approximately 7-9. Open-chain and cyclic imine products require separate analysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation, intermolecular imine formation with ammonia and cyanide capture could produce ornithine alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE APPLICATION of Strecker chemistry to an unprotected aminoaldehyde system. Intramolecular five-membered imine formation can divert the carbon skeleton toward cyclic products rather than ornithine. Patel et al. (2015) discuss competing amino-nitrile branches; ornithine detection in discharge samples supplies product-level context but not validation of this specific edge (Johnson et al., 2008; Parker et al., 2011).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-chain guanidinylation of ornithine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH screening within approximately 8-10; alpha-, delta- and multiple guanidinylation products must be resolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Desired addition of the terminal side-chain amine to cyanamide produces the arginine aminonitrile; competing alpha-amino-group reaction remains possible.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE AND NONSELECTIVE UNTIL TESTED. Related aliphatic-amine guanidinylation is supported by Patel et al. (2015), but no supplied study establishes delta-selective guanidinylation of this diamino nitrile. The two amines have different protonation and nucleophilicity behavior, which cannot be assumed to favor the desired site. This timing is included to test whether nitrile-stage functionalization can precede potentially destructive hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of ornithine alpha-aminonitrile to ornithinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Specific-acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic aqueous interval, approximately pH 1-3. An amide-accumulation window has not been established for this substrate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation and water addition, followed by tautomerization, yield the corresponding primary carboxamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE SUBSTRATE-SPECIFIC APPLICATION of ordinary nitrile hydration. No supplied study establishes selective ornithinamide accumulation under these conditions. The amide is included because it is a chemically relevant intermediate in nitrile hydrolysis, not an assumed new carbon-skeleton source. Related aminonitrile formation in Patel et al. (2015) does not establish downstream hydrolysis behavior.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of arginine alpha-aminonitrile to argininamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Specific-acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH approximately 1-3; seek nitrile hydration while monitoring guanidino-group integrity and other degradation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed addition of water to the alpha-aminonitrile produces argininamide through an imidic-acid intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"UNVERIFIED ARGININE-ENDGAME STEP. Patel et al. (2015) report the aminonitrile precursor, not its conversion to free arginine. Generic nitrile chemistry makes hydration chemically reasonable, but the supplied literature provides neither a substrate-specific yield nor a demonstrated selectivity window. This reaction must not be treated as experimentally secured merely because the preceding aminonitrile is reported.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Side-chain guanidinylation of ornithinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate, mildly alkaline interval after acidic nitrile hydration; approximately pH 8-10 screened. Regioselectivity is not assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Desired cyanamide addition at the terminal side-chain amine yields argininamide, competing with guanidinylation of the alpha-amino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE GUANIDINYLATION TIMING. This tests whether conversion of the nitrile to a carboxamide changes useful reactivity or selectivity before final carboxamide hydrolysis. Patel et al. (2015) provide related amine-cyanamide precedent. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrate ornithine-side-chain guanidination in a synthetic protein with a laboratory reagent, not this free amide with cyanamide; that result cannot establish this edge.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of ornithinamide to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Specific-acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed prolonged acidic aqueous interval, approximately pH 1-3; avoid drying or carboxyl activation. Ammonia represents the protonation-independent nitrogen coproduct.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to the protonated carboxamide, followed by tetrahedral-intermediate collapse, liberates the carboxylic acid and ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE PREBIOTIC APPLICATION of standard amide hydrolysis; no substrate-specific kinetics are supplied. This separates skeleton assembly from subsequent guanidinylation. Lactam formation should be monitored, particularly during drying or activation. Thoma and Powner (2023) establish a serious cyclization problem in ornithine peptide-ligation chemistry, but those observations do not directly establish the rate or outcome of this aqueous amide hydrolysis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of argininamide to free arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Specific-acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic hydrolysis window, approximately pH 1-3. Product accumulation requires carboxamide cleavage to outpace guanidino-group degradation; that window is unknown.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed carboxamide hydrolysis could release free arginine while retaining the side-chain guanidino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"UNVERIFIED TARGET-FORMING BOTTLENECK. The supplied literature does not demonstrate this step as the completion of a prebiotic arginine synthesis, and Patel et al. (2015) stop at precursor chemistry. Harsh or prolonged hydrolysis can also damage guanidino functionality. Free arginine, citrulline and ornithine would need independently resolved modern analysis; neither a yield nor successful accumulation is asserted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Late delta-guanidinylation of free ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated aqueous reactants; proposed pH screening within approximately 8-10. No scandium triflate, protecting group or activated laboratory guanidinylating reagent is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Desired attack of ornithine's delta-amino group on cyanamide forms the guanidino side chain of arginine. Alpha-guanidinylation, multiple modification and cyanamide self-reaction are competing possibilities.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE UNDER THE STATED PREBIOTIC CONDITIONS. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrate protein-bound ornithine-to-arginine guanidination with a laboratory reagent. Tsubokura et al. (2014) provide aqueous cyanamide guanylation precedent using Sc(OTf)3, which is deliberately excluded here as geochemically unjustified. Neither establishes efficient, delta-selective conversion of free ornithine by uncatalyzed cyanamide. This endpoint avoids exposing an already formed guanidino group to the preceding nitrile and amide hydrolysis stages.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidinylation with sulfide photochemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Guanidinylate the C3 aminonitrile before chain extension, then form and hydrolyze the arginine aminonitrile. This is a conditional cyanosulfidic variant, blocked by the unresolved homologation and unverified selective hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidinylation with sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"The early-guanidinylation ordering paired with an alternative electron-generation system. Distinct from P1 by redox inputs and mediator, but additionally requires an unestablished replacement for sulfur-dependent deoxygenation chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-stage guanidinylation after sulfide-driven extension\",\n      \"description\": \"Attempt carbon-chain extension with a free terminal amine, then guanidinylate before introducing the alpha-amino group. This could reduce later regioselectivity problems, but free-aminoaldehyde cyclization and cyanamide-aldehyde capture are major competing processes.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-stage guanidinylation after sulfite-driven extension\",\n      \"description\": \"The same guanidinylation timing as P3, but with a sulfite/ferrocyanide electron source. Both the free-amine extension and a compatible deoxygenation mechanism remain hypothetical.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminonitrile-stage guanidinylation with sulfide photochemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Construct the ornithine aminonitrile before attempting delta-guanidinylation and hydrolysis. In addition to skeleton construction, selective discrimination between alpha- and delta-amino groups is an unresolved bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminonitrile-stage guanidinylation with sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"The P5 ordering under an alternative proposed photoreductant system. This remains a conditional route variant rather than independent evidence for arginine synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Carboxamide-stage guanidinylation with sulfide photochemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrate the ornithine aminonitrile to ornithinamide before guanidinylation, then hydrolyze argininamide. This tests whether separating nitrile hydration from guanidinylation offers a useful reaction window; no such window or delta selectivity has been demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Carboxamide-stage guanidinylation with sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"The carboxamide-stage ordering using the alternative photoreductant hypothesis. It retains the unknown guanidinylation selectivity and final hydrolysis selectivity, while adding the unverified sulfite-compatible chain-extension requirement.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Free-ornithine guanidinylation after sulfide-driven precursor assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete nitrile and carboxamide hydrolysis before installing the guanidino group. This avoids hydrolytic exposure of guanidinylated intermediates, but neither the modeled ornithine construction nor selective catalyst-free cyanamide guanidinylation is established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Free-ornithine guanidinylation after sulfite-driven precursor assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"The late-guanidinylation ordering paired with the sulfite/ferrocyanide hypothesis. This is the tenth distinct graph route, not a tenth established synthesis; its carbon-chain assembly and target-forming guanidinylation remain experimental questions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_016\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_018\",\n    \"mol_020\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Arginine, with no assumed enantioselectivity", "overall_strategy": "Construct a target-directed hypothesis network from HCN, acetylene, ammonia and cyanamide. The supplied literature does not establish a complete prebiotic synthesis of free arginine. Accordingly, the ten pathways below are explicitly conditional variants: five possible timings of side-chain guanidinylation, each paired with either sulfide-driven or sulfite/ferrocyanide-driven photochemistry. They are not ten independently demonstrated syntheses. Early guanidinylation minimizes competition from the eventual alpha-amino group; later guanidinylation postpones exposure of the guanidino group to hydrolysis conditions. The crucial carbon-chain-extension edges are explicitly labeled unresolved, lumped transformations rather than established elementary reactions. Their atom balance does not establish their chemical feasibility. Numerical condition ranges are proposed screening windows, not verified reproductions of published protocols. All chemistry is anoxic and assigned to surface settings; no unsupported hydrothermal transfer is introduced. Chiral products have unspecified stereochemistry and would generally be racemic. Protonation equilibria, transient species and competing-product networks are not separately enumerated. Sulfate and elemental sulfur are bookkeeping coproducts labeled intermediate because the required role vocabulary lacks a byproduct category. The network exceeds the requested minimum size to represent chemically distinct intermediates without duplicating molecule nodes.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "Ethyne", "common_name": "Acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "Cyanamide", "common_name": "Cyanamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "SO3^2-", "iupac_name": "Sulfite", "common_name": "Sulfite ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "SO4^2-", "iupac_name": "Sulfate", "common_name": "Sulfate ion", "inchi": null, "role": "inte