mino_acids_and_evolution_of_the_triplet_code)\n  33. Order of amino acid recruitment into the genetic code resolved by last universal common ancestor\u2019s protein domains (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2410311121)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.4\n- Feedback: NOT APPROVED at the network threshold of 0.90. Structural validity is high: the 20 molecule nodes and 17 reaction nodes form a connected network, molecule IDs are consistent, and the stated pathways provide formal routes to the target. Sulfate and sulfur are legitimate terminal coproduct nodes, not isolated subgraphs. The deterministic check is accepted as ground truth: 13 reactions are balanced as written, four are balanceable with coefficients, and none violates atom or charge conservation. Explicit intended coefficients and atom mappings are still needed, particularly for rxn_004 and rxn_006; their unusual inferred coefficients are non-unique bookkeeping solutions, not mechanistic evidence. The decisive limitation is that every route requires one of the unresolved scaffold-construction edges rxn_004-rxn_007. Downstream branches additionally require unverified selectivity, hydrolysis conditions, or both. Conventional hydration and hydrolysis are not intrinsically invalid, and arginine degradation should be measured rather than presumed unavoidable, but no demonstrated operating window closes the network. Environmental consistency is underdetermined: anoxic surface photochemistry is broadly plausible and no unsupported interenvironment transfer is specified, but actual reaction-specific pH, temperature, concentration, irradiation, catalyst availability/speciation, and processing protocols are missing despite the strategy's reference to screening ranges. Literature alignment is strongest in the explicit acknowledgement of uncertainty. However, the reaction entries lack direct citation-to-condition mapping. Patel et al. supports relevant precursors rather than the supplied complete synthesis; the sulfite/ferrocyanide precedent does not establish these substrate-specific homologations; and peptide guanidination does not validate rxn_017. Unspecified stereochemistry is appropriate for the target. Overall confidence is bottleneck-weighted for the complete network, not an average of individually plausible reactions or a reward for the number of alternatives. This is a useful experimental hypothesis network, but it does not yet establish production of free arginine.\n- Issues: ['This is the best-supported conditional variant, but not a validated complete synthesis. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) research supports acrylonitrile formation, ammonia addition, inefficient early guanidinylation, and a downstream Strecker conversion. Early guanidinylation reasonably avoids competition from an alpha-amino group. However, rxn_004 does not specify an executable chain-extension sequence. Its balance does not establish selective formation of mol_014. Nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are chemically conventional, but their rates, yields, and compatibility with guanidino-group survival are unspecified. Surface photochemistry is plausible in principle; reaction-specific operating conditions are absent.', 'Early guanidinylation has a defensible selectivity rationale. The sulfite/ferrocyanide system has photoreductant precedent in Xu et al. (2018), as summarized in the supplied research, but that does not demonstrate rxn_005 on this multifunctional substrate. Replacing a photoreductant also does not automatically replace any sulfur-dependent substrate chemistry in a homologation sequence. Ferrocyanide is mentioned at pathway level without a reaction-specific loading, role, or operating protocol. The final hydrolysis sequence remains plausible but unvalidated under stated environmental conditions.', 'Guanidinylation before alpha-amino-group installation avoids one important selectivity problem. Nevertheless, rxn_006 leaves the essential carbon-skeleton construction unresolved while the terminal amine remains unprotected. Relevant aminoaldehyde intermediates can cyclize, and cyanohydrin equilibration can expose aldehydes to competing cyanamide chemistry. Therefore, rxn_008 cannot be assumed to give only the named guanidinylated cyanohydrin. Patel et al. (2015) supplies related chemistry, not validation of this particular sequence. The final hydration and hydrolysis steps require substrate-specific conditions and product-survival measurements.', 'This pathway combines unresolved free-amine scaffold construction with an unverified application of sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry. Rxn_007 is balanceable, but neither its carbon-bond-forming sequence nor its product selectivity is established. The general photoredox precedent does not demonstrate formation of mol_013. Cyanohydrin-stage guanidinylation is a reasonable hypothesis, although aldehyde trapping, cyclization, and cyanamide side reactions require testing. No contradiction follows merely from assigning every step to the surface, but the supplied conditions are insufficient to assess operational compatibility or the final hydrolysis.', 'The formal sequence reaches arginine, but the chemistry is interrupted by unresolved rxn_006. Rxn_010 is a plausible net Strecker transformation through aldehyde/imine equilibria, not a demonstrated selective conversion of this free-amine cyanohydrin. The corresponding aminoaldehyde can form a five-membered cyclic imine, potentially diverting material toward proline-type precursors. Rxn_011 then requires discrimination between the alpha- and terminal amino groups. Their differing electronic environments may help, but do not establish delta-selective cyanamide guanidinylation. Subsequent hydrolysis remains condition-dependent.', 'This route has the unresolved scaffold-construction, cyclic-imine competition, and aminonitrile guanidinylation-selectivity problems of P5. Additionally, the supplied sulfite/ferrocyanide precedent does not establish rxn_007 for this substrate or replace a specified homologation mechanism. Atom and charge conservation pass, but conservation alone cannot identify a route to the intended constitutional structure. The surface setting is broadly credible for photochemistry, while catalyst participation and the conditions connecting photochemistry, Strecker conversion, guanidinylation, and hydrolysis remain unspecified.', 'Hydrating the aminonitrile before guanidinylation avoids exposing a guanidino group to the nitrile-hydration stage. That is a chemically meaningful design consideration, but it does not resolve scaffold formation or free-aminoaldehyde cyclization. Rxn_012 is conventional in reaction class, yet selective accumulation of ornithinamide requires an operating window. Rxn_014 still presents two amino sites and lacks demonstrated delta selectivity. Argininamide-to-arginine hydrolysis is plausible, but no conditions or substrate-specific yields establish the endgame.', 'The amide-stage strategy is formally coherent and postpones guanidino-group exposure until after nitrile hydration. Nevertheless, rxn_007 remains an unsupported lumped transformation, and the sulfite/ferrocyanide literature does not establish this complete substrate conversion. Open-chain Strecker formation, controlled amide accumulation, and selective side-chain guanidinylation each need verification. The missing environmental specifications limit assessment, rather than proving that an all-surface sequence is intrinsically incompatible.', 'Late guanidinylation avoids the requirement that a guanidino group survive precursor hydrolysis. However, the supplied network does not establish ornithine production through rxn_006 and rxn_010, and rxn_017 is itself a decisive unresolved endgame. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrates guanidination of ornithine residues in a synthetic protein using a laboratory reagent, not selective cyanamide conversion of free ornithine under the proposed conditions. General guanidinylation precedent supports plausibility but does not establish rate, yield, or alpha/delta selectivity for this reaction.', 'This route postpones guanidinylation until hydrolysis is complete, but leaves both precursor construction and the target-forming reaction unresolved. Sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry is a supported general electron-generation system, not evidence for the specific chain construction in rxn_007. The final cyanamide reaction requires demonstrated free-ornithine delta selectivity and a practical rate. Consequently, reaching mol_020 in the graph does not establish chemical target achievement. All reactions pass the supplied conservation check; the limitations concern mechanism, selectivity, conditions, and evidence.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The supplied evidence does not establish any complete prebiotic synthesis of free arginine, much less ten independently validated routes. Accordingly, this is a conditional research network containing ten distinct reaction sequences, not ten demonstrated syntheses. Nine sequences test different chemically meaningful positions for guanidinylation and different nitrile-processing routes; the tenth uses the experimentally reported, low-abundance ornithine inventory from discharge chemistry. To avoid inventing the unresolved structural details between Patel's reported intermediates, the explicit scaffold-construction hypothesis is acetylene hydrocyanation, cyanide conjugate addition to acrylonitrile, and selective hydrogenation of one succinonitrile terminus. This alternative avoids an unspecified carbon-chain-extension or deoxygenation step, but is less experimentally supported than Patel's published precursor chemistry and must not be represented as its reconstruction. Early guanidinylation could suppress aminoaldehyde cyclization; hydantoin or N-alpha-carbamoyl intermediates could mask the alpha nitrogen during later guanidinylation. Neither advantage establishes an operating window or yield. All numerical conditions below are proposed screening ranges, not literature-reproduced protocols; reaction reasoning distinguishes direct evidence, analogy, and speculation. The hydrothermal hydrogenation branch requires surface-derived succinonitrile to enter a hydrogen-rich circulation zone and the product to return to a surface photochemical setting; that transport is an additional unverified requirement. Conditions are anoxic throughout. Reduced iron may sequester cyanide or sulfide and must be included in compatibility tests rather than silently excluded. Formulas represent neutral reference forms, not the predominant aqueous protonation states. Complex InChIs are null where not independently verified. Sulfur is an idealized redox-accounting coproduct, not an experimentally established exclusive sulfur product. No pathway establishes selective L-arginine formation, and no complete-network confidence near 0.90 is justified by the supplied evidence.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"commo