"role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "C6H14N4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-5-(carbamimidamido)pentanoic acid", "common_name": "Arginine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_027", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "Cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_028", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "Methane", "common_name": "Methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Copper-mediated hydrocyanation of acetylene", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I) complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I) complexes; proposed total copper 10-100 micromolar", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic gas-water interface; proposed pH 6-8, dissolved HCN 1-50 mM, and continuously replenished acetylene. Measure soluble copper speciation and cyanide binding by Fe2+. No UV is assumed necessary for this addition."}, "mechanism": "Copper-mediated addition of HCN across the acetylene triple bond. Intended molecular stoichiometry is 1:1:1. The HCN carbon becomes the nitrile carbon, while both acetylene carbons become the vinyl carbons.", "reasoning": "Transformation-level support: Patel et al. (2015) identify Cu(I)-mediated acetylene/HCN chemistry as the acrylonitrile entry. The supplied report does not provide its exact recipe, so the numerical conditions here are screening hypotheses, not attributed experimental conditions. Copper availability and cyanide sequestration remain geochemical constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Proposed cyanide conjugate addition producing a C4 dinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, acrylonitrile 1-20 mM, and total HCN/CN- 10-100 mM; dark, anoxic incubation for hours to days. Determine free cyanide after Fe2+ complexation. Quantify acrylonitrile oligomers rather than assuming exclusive hydrocyanation."}, "mechanism": "CN- attacks the terminal vinyl carbon of acrylonitrile; protonation of the resulting carbanion gives succinonitrile. Intended net: acrylonitrile + HCN -> succinonitrile. The incoming HCN carbon becomes the second nitrile carbon.", "reasoning": "Speculative for the proposed prebiotic mixture; no supporting experiment for this edge is supplied. This conventional conjugate-addition hypothesis is included because it makes C4 skeleton construction explicit without an unexplained homologation or oxygen-removal step. Patel et al. (2015) instead establish ammonia addition to acrylonitrile; that result does not demonstrate cyanide addition, its yield, or competition with oligomerization."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Proposed selective monohydrogenation of succinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Nickel-rich native Ni-Fe alloy surface, such as awaruite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "50-150 bar total; proposed H2 partial pressure 10-50 bar", "catalyst": "Exposed Ni-rich metallic sites on a native Ni-Fe alloy; substrate-specific activity unverified", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-10 and succinonitrile 1-10 mM, in an H2-rich cool hydrothermal mixing zone. Screen residence times from minutes to days. Track diamine formation, imines, secondary amines, sulfur poisoning, and metal passivation. Surface-to-subsurface delivery is required."}, "mechanism": "Surface-dissociated hydrogen reduces one nitrile through imine intermediates to a primary amine while leaving the other nitrile intact. Intended net: succinonitrile + 2 H2 -> 4-aminobutanenitrile. Either symmetry-equivalent nitrile can be reduced.", "reasoning": "Speculative: neither this substrate-selective conversion nor its operation on awaruite is demonstrated in the supplied research. Nickel is chosen for its established chemical capacity to activate H2 and hydrogenate nitriles, not because a mineral name establishes prebiotic activity. Selective stopping after one nitrile reduction is a major unresolved scaffold bottleneck; full hydrogenation and catalyst poisoning may dominate. This edge is not supported by the cited keto-acid amination studies."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Early guanidinylation of the C4 amino nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, amino nitrile 1-10 mM, cyanamide 20-200 mM, and days-to-weeks residence in a concentrated pool. Separate from sulfide-rich irradiation stages. Quantify cyanamide dimerization and ammonia-derived guanidine."}, "mechanism": "The terminal primary amine attacks cyanamide's nitrile carbon, followed by proton transfer to form an N-substituted guanidine. Net addition is 1:1, with no small-molecule coproduct.", "reasoning": "Substrate-specific hypothesis. Patel et al. (2015) report inefficient cyanamide guanidinylation of the one-carbon-shorter beta-aminopropionitrile, providing reaction-class precedent but not a yield for this substrate. Performing guanidinylation before installing an alpha amine removes alpha/delta competition and could suppress later cyclic-imine formation. Those advantages do not establish adequate rate or conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Proposed photoreduction of the unprotected C4 amino nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_007", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003", "mol_027"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "Phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox complexes; proposed total copper 10-100 micromolar", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7-8, substrate 1-10 mM, total sulfide 1-10 mM, and phosphate 5-50 mM. Laboratory 254 nm irradiation at 0.01-0.1 mW/cm2 for 1-24 hours is a test condition, not an established solar equivalent. Quantify copper-sulfide precipitation and cyclic-imine products."}, "mechanism": "Two-electron nitrile reduction gives an imine, whose hydrolysis releases ammonia and the aldehyde. Formal atom-balanced redox projection: amino nitrile + H2S + H2O -> aminoaldehyde + NH3 + 1/8 S8. The nitrile carbon becomes the aldehyde carbon.", "reasoning": "Analogy only: Patel et al. (2015) report photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile, not this C4 substrate under these conditions. The resulting 4-aminobutanal can form a five-membered cyclic imine, making productive open-chain capture uncertain. Ranjan et al. (2018) emphasize the conditional availability of surface sulfide. S8 is an idealized electron-balance endpoint; actual sulfur speciation and yield must be measured."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Proposed photoreduction of the guanidinylated C4 nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_007", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003", "mol_027"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "Phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox complexes; proposed total copper 10-100 micromolar", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7-8, guanidinylated nitrile 1-10 mM, sulfide 1-10 mM, and phosphate 5-50 mM. Screen 254 nm irradiation at 0.01-0.1 mW/cm2 for 1-24 hours. Verify guanidino-group survival, copper speciation, and aldehyde accumulation."}, "mechanism": "Partial nitrile reduction followed by imine hydrolysis. Intended net: guanidinylated nitrile + H2S + H2O -> guanidinoaldehyde + NH3 + 1/8 S8. The guanidine carbon is retained unchanged.", "reasoning": "Speculative application of the photoreduction class in Patel et al. (2015), not a reproduction of their integrated arginine branch. Guanidinylation plausibly decreases terminal-nitrogen nucleophilicity and cyclic-imine trapping, but may also alter metal binding and photochemistry. The supplied report establishes neither substrate-specific kinetics nor the proposed numerical conditions. Sulfur products remain experimentally unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Cyanohydrin capture of the unprotected aminoaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 5.5-7, transient aminoaldehyde below 5 mM, and HCN/CN- 20-100 mM. Test immediate capture after photoreduction and compare with delayed addition. Acidification may suppress amine cyclization but also reduces the cyanide-anion fraction."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl. The incoming HCN carbon becomes the future amino-acid carboxyl carbon; the aldehyde carbon becomes the future alpha carbon.", "reasoning": "Speculative for this multifunctional substrate and mixture. Cyanohydrin chemistry is conventional, but the supplied research identifies unprotected aminoaldehyde cyclization as a central obstacle. General Strecker precedent does not establish interception of 4-aminobutanal before cyclic-imine formation. No selective yield is assigned."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Cyanohydrin capture of the guanidinoaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 6.5-8, aldehyde 1-5 mM, and HCN/CN- 20-100 mM; dark incubation for minutes to hours. Determine equilibrium conversion and aldehyde loss independently."}, "mechanism": "Reversible nucleophilic cyanide addition gives the cyanohydrin. The HCN carbon supplies the future carboxyl carbon; no carbon-chain rearrangement is assumed.", "reasoning": "The supplied account of Patel et al. (2015) includes a cyanohydrin upstream of the arginine aminonitrile. That supports this type of terminal functionality, not the upstream succinonitrile route or the proposed conditions. Exact identification against the primary structure of intermediate 45 and substrate-specific equilibrium measurements remain necessary."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Proposed Strecker conversion to ornithine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-9.5, cyanohydrin 1-10 mM, and total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1 M; hours-to-days incubation. Maintain cyanide in the reaction pool and measure open-chain versus cyclic products."}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin dissociation exposes aldehyde and cyanide; ammonia forms an imine or iminium species, which cyanide captures. Net: cyanohydrin + NH3 -> aminonitrile + H2O. This is not direct displacement of hydroxide.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific Strecker reaction. Discharge detection of ornithine in Johnson et al. (2008) and Parker et al. (2011) does not identify this intermediate or establish this mechanism. Re-formation of an unprotected aminoaldehyde exposes the branch to cyclic-imine competition even if cyanohydrin capture initially succeeds."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Strecker conversion toward arginine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-9.5, cyanohydrin 1-10 mM, and total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1 M; hours-to-days incubation. These values are not the recovered experimental recipe for Patel's 45-to-46 conversion."}, "mechanism": "Aldehyde/imine/cyanohydrin equilibria followed by cyanide addition install the alpha amino group. Net: guanidinylated cyanohydrin + NH3 -> arginine aminonitrile + H2O.", "reasoning": "This is the closest terminal precursor transformation to the reported evidence: the supplied summary of Patel et al. (2015) lists a 45-to-46 conversion to arginine aminonitrile at 90%. That reported yield is not assigned to the conditions here, nor to the entire pathway. Integrated formation was conditional, and free arginine was not reported."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Proposed delta guanidinylation of ornithine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, aminonitrile 1-10 mM, cyanamide 20-200 mM, and days-to-weeks incubation. Resolve alpha-, delta-, and bis-guanidinylated products by orthogonal analytical methods."}, "mechanism": "Desired terminal-amine addition to cyanamide. Intended net is a 1:1 addition retaining the alpha amine and nitrile.", "reasoning": "Speculative. Patel et al. (2015) provide simple-amine guanidinylation precedent, not delta selectivity in this diamino substrate. The alpha aminonitrile group changes basicity and nucleophilicity, but those changes do not establish which amine reacts preferentially. Cyanamide dimerization and aminonitrile decomposition are additional competing processes."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Proposed controlled hydration of ornithine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from acidic volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-360 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2 and substrate 1-10 mM; sample over hours to days. Test whether amide accumulates before further hydrolysis, retro-Strecker chemistry, or decomposition. This acid stage is separate from alkaline guanidinylation."}, "mechanism": "Acid activation of the nitrile, water addition, and tautomerization yield the primary amide. Net: aminonitrile + H2O -> aminoamide.", "reasoning": "Speculative for this substrate and operating window. Nitrile hydration is conventional chemistry, but the provided studies do not establish controlled ornithinamide production. Acidic volcanic waters provide a possible catalyst source, not evidence for selectivity or a useful rate."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Proposed hydration of arginine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from acidic volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-360 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2 and aminonitrile 1-10 mM; time-resolved sampling over hours to days. Quantify argininamide, residual nitrile, retro-Strecker products, and guanidino-group degradation."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration gives argininamide without intentionally changing the guanidine group. Net: arginine aminonitrile + H2O -> argininamide.", "reasoning": "Unverified target-completion step. Patel et al. (2015) do not report hydrolysis of their arginine aminonitrile to free arginine. Conventional nitrile hydration supports plausibility, but neither a useful rate nor guanidino-group survival follows from that precedent. The proposed range is an experiment to locate an operating window, not evidence that one exists."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Proposed delta guanidinylation of ornithinamide", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, ornithinamide 1-10 mM, and cyanamide 20-200 mM; days-to-weeks incubation after neutralizing the hydration-stage solution. Measure regioisomer distributions."}, "mechanism": "Desired side-chain amine addition to cyanamide forms argininamide; the alpha amine and carboxamide remain unchanged.", "reasoning": "Speculative. Postponing guanidinylation until after nitrile hydration reduces the exposure of guanidine to that earlier stage, but leaves alpha/delta competition unresolved. Patel et al. (2015) support only inefficient guanidinylation of a simpler amine. Longo et al. (2020) do not establish cyanamide selectivity on free ornithinamide."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Proposed hydrolysis of ornithinamide to ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from acidic volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2 and amide 1-10 mM; screen 1-30 days without assuming completion. Monitor lactam formation and carbon-skeleton degradation. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated carboxamide; collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases ammonia/ammonium and forms the carboxylic acid. Net: ornithinamide + H2O -> ornithine + NH3.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific application of ordinary amide hydrolysis. The supplied literature does not provide this operating window. Ornithine-related lactamization is a relevant warning from Thoma and Powner (2023), although their activated peptide-formation results do not directly measure hydrolysis of this free amide."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Unverified hydrolysis of argininamide to free arginine", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_026", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from acidic volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2, argininamide 1-10 mM, and 1-30 days. Establish an actual target-accumulation window by separately measuring formation and destruction rates. Confirm arginine against an authentic standard using chromatography and mass spectrometry."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of the carboxamide. Intended net: argininamide + H2O -> arginine + NH3. Guanidino-group hydrolysis is a competing, not desired, reaction.", "reasoning": "Speculative and decisive for target achievement. Patel et al. (2015) stop at the aminonitrile and do not validate this endgame. The supplied research warns that harsh hydrolysis can damage guanidino functionality. Such damage should be quantified rather than presumed unavoidable, but no demonstrated selective operating window is available."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Unverified selective cyanamide guanidinylation of free ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, ornithine 1-10 mM, cyanamide 20-200 mM, and days-to-weeks incubation. Quantify alpha-, delta-, and bis-guanidinylation, cyanamide dimerization, and ornithine loss. No scandium triflate or activated laboratory guanidinylating reagent is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Desired addition of the delta amine to cyanamide forms the side-chain guanidine. Net: ornithine + cyanamide -> arginine.", "reasoning": "Unverified prebiotic endgame. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrate guanidination of ornithine residues in a synthetic protein using a laboratory reagent, not selective cyanamide conversion of free ornithine. The cyanamide/Sc(OTf)3 precedent summarized for Tsubokura et al. (2014) does not establish an uncatalyzed prebiotic process. The slower cyanamide chemistry in Patel et al. (2015) reinforces the rate concern. This edge must not be labeled demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Proposed Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of ornithine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9.5, aminonitrile 1-10 mM, total dissolved inorganic carbon 50-500 mM, and ammonia/ammonium 0.1-0.5 M. CO2 denotes the carbon source across its aqueous acid-base forms. Screen hours to days."}, "mechanism": "Alpha-amine carbamation, intramolecular nitrile capture, and rearrangement produce a hydantoin. Formal net: ornithine aminonitrile + CO2 -> ornithine hydantoin. The added CO2 carbon becomes the ureide carbonyl; the nitrile carbon becomes the other ring carbonyl.", "reasoning": "Speculative extension of conventional Bucherer-Bergs chemistry; no substrate-specific study is supplied. The route is included because it could mask the alpha nitrogen before guanidinylation rather than merely add another nominal pathway. Side-chain carbamation, cyclic-imine chemistry, and hydantoin yield require testing."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Proposed hydantoin formation from arginine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_023"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9.5, aminonitrile 1-10 mM, dissolved inorganic carbon 50-500 mM, and ammonia/ammonium 0.1-0.5 M; hours-to-days incubation. Determine guanidino-group compatibility."}, "mechanism": "Bucherer-Bergs-type cyclization incorporates CO2 into the hydantoin ureide carbonyl while converting the nitrile carbon into the second carbonyl. Formal net: arginine aminonitrile + CO2 -> arginine hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Speculative; no supporting experiment for this substrate appears in the supplied research. This is a chemically distinct nitrile-processing alternative to direct hydration, not evidence that target release is easier. It adds a subsequent decarbamoylation requirement and may be less favorable overall."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Proposed side-chain guanidinylation with alpha nitrogen masked in a hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_023"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, ornithine hydantoin 1-10 mM, and cyanamide 20-200 mM; days-to-weeks incubation. Quantify side-chain addition, ring-nitrogen modification, and hydantoin hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "The exposed terminal primary amine adds to cyanamide. The alpha nitrogen remains incorporated in the hydantoin ring. Net addition is 1:1.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific guanidinylation. Unlike free ornithine, this substrate has its alpha nitrogen acylated within a ring, giving a mechanistic rationale for improved side-chain discrimination. Patel et al. (2015) support the underlying simple-amine/cyanamide reaction class, not the rate or selectivity here. Hydantoin nitrogens are not assumed completely inert."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Proposed ring opening of ornithine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions from alkaline pool water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-365 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "General-base/hydroxide-assisted hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 10.5-12 and hydantoin 1-10 mM; sample over hours to days. Alkalinity must be sufficient to maintain pH as carboxylate forms. Measure ring-opening regioselectivity and subsequent decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis at the hydantoin carbonyl derived from the original nitrile opens the ring to N-alpha-carbamoylornithine. Neutral-reference net: hydantoin + H2O -> N-carbamoyl amino acid.", "reasoning": "Speculative for this substrate and environmental window; the supplied research contains no hydantoin hydrolysis experiment for ornithine. The reaction class is conventional, but selective accumulation of the N-alpha-carbamoyl product must be established. Buffer proton transfers are omitted from the neutral-reference equation, not from experimental pH requirements."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Proposed ring opening of arginine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_025"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide ions from alkaline pool water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-365 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "General-base/hydroxide-assisted hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 10.5-12 and hydantoin 1-10 mM; hours-to-days monitoring. Measure both ring opening and competing side-chain guanidine hydrolysis. Do not equate disappearance of starting material with productive conversion."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the appropriate ring amide bond gives N-alpha-carbamoylarginine. Neutral-reference net: arginine hydantoin + H2O -> N-alpha-carbamoylarginine.", "reasoning": "Speculative; no substrate-specific evidence is supplied. This ordinary reaction class does not establish selective arginine-precursor accumulation. Guanidino-group survival under alkaline heating is a separate requirement, and the additional decarbamoylation step remains unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Proposed guanidinylation of N-alpha-carbamoylornithine", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_025"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8.5-10, substrate 1-10 mM, and cyanamide 20-200 mM; days-to-weeks incubation after adjusting the ring-opening mixture. Avoid assuming quantitative alpha protection or selective side-chain conversion."}, "mechanism": "The free delta amine attacks cyanamide while the alpha nitrogen remains in a carbamoyl group. Net: N-alpha-carbamoylornithine + cyanamide -> N-alpha-carbamoylarginine.", "reasoning": "Speculative. Carbamoylation lowers alpha-nitrogen nucleophilicity and provides a chemically meaningful selectivity hypothesis without an imported synthetic protecting group. Patel et al. (2015) supply only general cyanamide guanidinylation precedent. The benefit must be weighed against the later need to remove one ureido group while preserving the side-chain guanidine."}, {"id": "rxn_024", "name": "Proposed decarbamoylation to ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from acidic volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2 and substrate 1-10 mM; screen 1-30 days. Quantify hydantoin re-formation, decarbamoylation, lactamization, and total carbon recovery. CO2 escape is possible but not assumed to guarantee conversion."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the N-carbamoyl group releases the alpha amine; the remaining carbamic-acid fragment decomposes to ammonia and CO2. Net: N-alpha-carbamoylornithine + H2O -> ornithine + NH3 + CO2.", "reasoning": "Speculative for these conditions and substrate. No supporting experiment is provided. This branch removes the alpha mask before guanidinylation, avoiding exposure of an already installed guanidine to decarbamoylation conditions, but forfeiting the mask's potential regioselectivity benefit."}, {"id": "rxn_025", "name": "Unverified selective decarbamoylation releasing arginine", "inputs": ["mol_025", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_026", "mol_003", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions from acidic volcanic water"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K; proposed screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 1-2 and precursor 1-10 mM; screen 1-30 days with independent arginine-survival controls. Resolve arginine, carbamoylated precursor, hydantoin, citrulline-related products, and ornithine rather than relying on a single amino-acid-analyzer peak."}, "mechanism": "Desired hydrolysis of the alpha N-carbamoyl substituent, followed by carbamic-acid decomposition. Net: N-alpha-carbamoylarginine + H2O -> arginine + NH3 + CO2. Selective preservation of the side-chain guanidine is required.", "reasoning": "Speculative and a decisive bottleneck. No supplied study demonstrates this selective target-release reaction. The historical analytical disagreement between Matthews and Moser (1967) and the later Ferris studies (1973, 1974) underscores the need for orthogonal product identification, not support for this mechanism. Distinguishing productive decarbamoylation from guanidino-group damage requires measured kinetics."}, {"id": "rxn_026", "name": "Empirical aggregate source of trace ornithine from discharge chemistry", "inputs": ["mol_028", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K in the aqueous collector; the discharge channel is non-equilibrium and much hotter", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm; proposed local reducing-gas setting", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Electrical discharge over a CH4/NH3/H2O gas-water system, with condensate recirculation for days. This is a proposed envelope, not a reconstruction of archived Miller apparatus conditions. Exact gas ratios, discharge power, workup, and trace-product recovery must be taken from primary experimental records before replication."}, "mechanism": "Empirical multireaction source edge, not an elementary or mechanistically resolved synthesis. A minimal neutral-form elemental projection is 5 CH4 + 2 NH3 + 2 H2O -> ornithine + 9 H2. These coefficients are bookkeeping, not measured product ratios, an atom mapping, or a claim that the complex discharge mixture follows this single net reaction.", "reasoning": "Product-level evidence: Johnson et al. (2008) and Parker et al. (2011) report ornithine in reanalyses of relevant reducing discharge samples, not arginine. This edge is included to distinguish an experimentally observed scaffold source from the untested succinonitrile construction. Its elementary mechanism and absolute productive flux remain unresolved, and a globally reducing early atmosphere is not assumed. The subsequent free-ornithine guanidinylation is still unverified."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Early guanidinylation followed by direct nitrile hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional route installing guanidine on the C4 amino nitrile before aminoaldehyde formation, followed by Strecker chemistry and stepwise nitrile-to-amide-to-acid hydrolysis. This avoids alpha/delta guanidinylation competition but requires the unverified scaffold construction and guanidine-compatible hydrolysis window.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_013", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_016", "mol_018", "mol_020"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Early guanidinylation followed by hydantoin processing", "description": "Conditional alternative to P1 in which arginine aminonitrile is captured as a hydantoin, opened, and decarbamoylated. This is a distinct nitrile-processing sequence, not a demonstrated improvement over direct hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_019", "rxn_022", "rxn_025"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_018", "mol_023", "mol_025"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Aminonitrile-stage guanidinylation followed by direct hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional route carrying an unprotected terminal amine through aldehyde capture and Strecker chemistry, then guanidinylating ornithine aminonitrile. Aminoaldehyde cyclization and alpha/delta selectivity are additional bottlenecks beyond scaffold construction and hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_013", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_015", "mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_020"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Aminonitrile-stage guanidinylation followed by hydantoin processing", "description": "Conditional counterpart of P3 using hydantoin formation after guanidinylation. The hydantoin does not solve the earlier guanidinylation selectivity problem and adds an unverified selective decarbamoylation step.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_019", "rxn_022", "rxn_025"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_017", "mol_018", "mol_023", "mol_025"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Carboxamide-stage guanidinylation", "description": "Conditional route hydrating ornithine aminonitrile before guanidinylation. It reduces guanidine exposure to nitrile hydration but still requires selective delta guanidinylation and argininamide hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_014", "rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_017", "mol_019", "mol_020"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Late guanidinylation after direct ornithine formation", "description": "Conditional route completing nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis before installing guanidine. It avoids guanidine exposure to those hydrolyses but depends on unverified selective cyanamide conversion of free ornithine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_015", "rxn_017"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_017", "mol_019", "mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Hydantoin-masked alpha nitrogen during guanidinylation", "description": "Conditional route converting ornithine aminonitrile to its hydantoin before side-chain guanidinylation. Alpha-nitrogen masking supplies a selectivity rationale, but subsequent guanidine-compatible ring opening and decarbamoylation remain unverified.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_018", "rxn_020", "rxn_022", "rxn_025"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_017", "mol_022", "mol_023", "mol_025"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "N-alpha-carbamoyl masking during guanidinylation", "description": "Conditional route opening ornithine hydantoin before guanidinylation while retaining an alpha carbamoyl mask. It tests side-chain selectivity in an open-chain masked amino acid, followed by the unresolved selective removal of that mask.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_018", "rxn_021", "rxn_023", "rxn_025"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_022", "mol_024", "mol_025"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Complete hydantoin hydrolysis before late guanidinylation", "description": "Conditional route removing the hydantoin