sumed unavoidable, but no demonstrated operating window closes the network. Environmental consistency is underdetermined: anoxic surface photochemistry is broadly plausible and no unsupported interenvironment transfer is specified, but actual reaction-specific pH, temperature, concentration, irradiation, catalyst availability/speciation, and processing protocols are missing despite the strategy's reference to screening ranges. Literature alignment is strongest in the explicit acknowledgement of uncertainty. However, the reaction entries lack direct citation-to-condition mapping. Patel et al. supports relevant precursors rather than the supplied complete synthesis; the sulfite/ferrocyanide precedent does not establish these substrate-specific homologations; and peptide guanidination does not validate rxn_017. Unspecified stereochemistry is appropriate for the target. Overall confidence is bottleneck-weighted for the complete network, not an average of individually plausible reactions or a reward for the number of alternatives. This is a useful experimental hypothesis network, but it does not yet establish production of free arginine.\n- Issues: ['This is the best-supported conditional variant, but not a validated complete synthesis. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) research supports acrylonitrile formation, ammonia addition, inefficient early guanidinylation, and a downstream Strecker conversion. Early guanidinylation reasonably avoids competition from an alpha-amino group. However, rxn_004 does not specify an executable chain-extension sequence. Its balance does not establish selective formation of mol_014. Nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are chemically conventional, but their rates, yields, and compatibility with guanidino-group survival are unspecified. Surface photochemistry is plausible in principle; reaction-specific operating conditions are absent.', 'Early guanidinylation has a defensible selectivity rationale. The sulfite/ferrocyanide system has photoreductant precedent in Xu et al. (2018), as summarized in the supplied research, but that does not demonstrate rxn_005 on this multifunctional substrate. Replacing a photoreductant also does not automatically replace any sulfur-dependent substrate chemistry in a homologation sequence. Ferrocyanide is mentioned at pathway level without a reaction-specific loading, role, or operating protocol. The final hydrolysis sequence remains plausible but unvalidated under stated environmental conditions.', 'Guanidinylation before alpha-amino-group installation avoids one important selectivity problem. Nevertheless, rxn_006 leaves the essential carbon-skeleton construction unresolved while the terminal amine remains unprotected. Relevant aminoaldehyde intermediates can cyclize, and cyanohydrin equilibration can expose aldehydes to competing cyanamide chemistry. Therefore, rxn_008 cannot be assumed to give only the named guanidinylated cyanohydrin. Patel et al. (2015) supplies related chemistry, not validation of this particular sequence. The final hydration and hydrolysis steps require substrate-specific conditions and product-survival measurements.', 'This pathway combines unresolved free-amine scaffold construction with an unverified application of sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry. Rxn_007 is balanceable, but neither its carbon-bond-forming sequence nor its product selectivity is established. The general photoredox precedent does not demonstrate formation of mol_013. Cyanohydrin-stage guanidinylation is a reasonable hypothesis, although aldehyde trapping, cyclization, and cyanamide side reactions require testing. No contradiction follows merely from assigning every step to the surface, but the supplied conditions are insufficient to assess operational compatibility or the final hydrolysis.', 'The formal sequence reaches arginine, but the chemistry is interrupted by unresolved rxn_006. Rxn_010 is a plausible net Strecker transformation through aldehyde/imine equilibria, not a demonstrated selective conversion of this free-amine cyanohydrin. The corresponding aminoaldehyde can form a five-membered cyclic imine, potentially diverting material toward proline-type precursors. Rxn_011 then requires discrimination between the alpha- and terminal amino groups. Their differing electronic environments may help, but do not establish delta-selective cyanamide guanidinylation. Subsequent hydrolysis remains condition-dependent.', 'This route has the unresolved scaffold-construction, cyclic-imine competition, and aminonitrile guanidinylation-selectivity problems of P5. Additionally, the supplied sulfite/ferrocyanide precedent does not establish rxn_007 for this substrate or replace a specified homologation mechanism. Atom and charge conservation pass, but conservation alone cannot identify a route to the intended constitutional structure. The surface setting is broadly credible for photochemistry, while catalyst participation and the conditions connecting photochemistry, Strecker conversion, guanidinylation, and hydrolysis remain unspecified.', 'Hydrating the aminonitrile before guanidinylation avoids exposing a guanidino group to the nitrile-hydration stage. That is a chemically meaningful design consideration, but it does not resolve scaffold formation or free-aminoaldehyde cyclization. Rxn_012 is conventional in reaction class, yet selective accumulation of ornithinamide requires an operating window. Rxn_014 still presents two amino sites and lacks demonstrated delta selectivity. Argininamide-to-arginine hydrolysis is plausible, but no conditions or substrate-specific yields establish the endgame.', 'The amide-stage strategy is formally coherent and postpones guanidino-group exposure until after nitrile hydration. Nevertheless, rxn_007 remains an unsupported lumped transformation, and the sulfite/ferrocyanide literature does not establish this complete substrate conversion. Open-chain Strecker formation, controlled amide accumulation, and selective side-chain guanidinylation each need verification. The missing environmental specifications limit assessment, rather than proving that an all-surface sequence is intrinsically incompatible.', 'Late guanidinylation avoids the requirement that a guanidino group survive precursor hydrolysis. However, the supplied network does not establish ornithine production through rxn_006 and rxn_010, and rxn_017 is itself a decisive unresolved endgame. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrates guanidination of ornithine residues in a synthetic protein using a laboratory reagent, not selective cyanamide conversion of free ornithine under the proposed conditions. General guanidinylation precedent supports plausibility but does not establish rate, yield, or alpha/delta selectivity for this reaction.', 'This route postpones guanidinylation until hydrolysis is complete, but leaves both precursor construction and the target-forming reaction unresolved. Sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry is a supported general electron-generation system, not evidence for the specific chain construction in rxn_007. The final cyanamide reaction requires demonstrated free-ornithine delta selectivity and a practical rate. Consequently, reaching mol_020 in the graph does not establish chemical target achievement. All reactions pass the supplied conservation check; the limitations concern mechanism, selectivity, conditions, and evidence.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.44\n- Feedback: Not approved as an established complete synthesis network; it is a chemically motivated research proposal. Structural validity is strong: the 28 molecule nodes and 26 reaction nodes form a connected network, molecule IDs are consistent, and the supplied deterministic check establishes no atom or charge conservation violations. Insert the reported coefficients for rxn_003, rxn_005, rxn_006 and rxn_026 explicitly. The eightfold scaling in the photoreductions follows from representing sulfur as S8 and is not itself suspicious; nevertheless, that balanced representation does not establish actual sulfur-product speciation. The central weaknesses are chemical and evidential: selective succinonitrile monoreduction is unsupported, unprotected aminoaldehyde branches face cyclization, several guanidinylations require unproven positional selectivity, and none of rxn_016, rxn_017 or rxn_025 has a demonstrated productive endpoint under the proposed environmental conditions. The strategy mentions numerical screening ranges, but none are actually supplied for the reaction nodes. Surface photochemistry and hydrogen-rich circulation are broadly conceivable, yet catalyst identity and availability, pH, temperature, hydrogen pressure, residence times and transfer survival remain unspecified. Copper, cyanide, sulfide and reduced-iron compatibility must be measured rather than assumed. Literature alignment is good in acknowledging these limitations and distinguishing the alternative scaffold from Patel's chemistry, but the supplied reaction entries lack reaction-specific citations and experimental conditions. The supplied Patel evidence supports precursor chemistry, not this complete alternative network; discharge studies support ornithine, and protein guanidination studies do not validate free-ornithine guanidinylation by cyanamide. Multiple proposed sequences do not independently establish target production. No penalty is warranted for unspecified stereochemistry because the requested target is stereochemically unspecified. The low overall score reflects the absence of an adequately supported feedstock-to-free-arginine route, not a demonstrated impossibility or any preference concerning feedstocks, hubs, reaction families or environmental ordering.\n- Issues: ['The carbon skeleton and functional-group sequence are coherent, and early guanidinylation could reduce aminoaldehyde cyclization without requiring alpha-versus-delta amine discrimination. However, rxn_003 lacks evidence for accumulation of the mono-reduced product rather than further reduction or cyclization. Patel et al. (2015), as summarized in the supplied research, support related guanidinylation, photoreduction and arginine-aminonitrile chemistry, not this substituted C4 scaffold-construction sequence. The final amide hydrolysis is chemically plausible, but neither a guanidino-preserving operating window nor free-arginine production is demonstrated. All reactions pass the supplied conservation check after coefficient insertion.', 'Early guanidinylation offers a defensible selectivity hypothesis, but the unvalidated succinonitrile reduction remains an upstream bottleneck. Bucherer-Bergs-type trapping is a reasonable reaction-family analogy; the balanced CO2 addition does not establish the necessary carbonate speciation, kinetics or yield for this substrate. Hydantoin ring opening and N-alpha-decarbamoylation are separate chemical requirements. In particular, rxn_025 needs evidence that the alpha-carbamoyl group can be removed productively while retaining the side-chain guanidino group. The supplied Patel evidence does not validate this hydantoin-processing branch or free-arginine release.', 'The formal route reaches the correct arginine precursor, but two important selectivity assumptions are unresolved. Unprotected gamma-aminobutyraldehyde can cyclize to a five-membered imine, so rxn_005, rxn_007 and rxn_009 cannot assume a persistent linear aldehyde inventory or exclusive linear Strecker chemistry. At rxn_011, both alpha and terminal amino groups are available, and selective terminal guanidinylation by cyanamide is not demonstrated. General Strecker and nitrile-hydrolysis precedent supports the reaction classes, not their integrated selectivity in this mixture. The upstream hydrogenation and final arginine-releasing hydrolysis also remain unvalidated.', 'Hydantoin formation occurs after guanidinylation here, so it does not solve the alpha-versus-delta selectivity problem in rxn_011. The preceding unprotected aminoaldehyde sequence also remains vulnerable to cyclic-imine and cyclic Strecker products. Downstream hydantoin chemistry is plausible by analogy, but neither selective processing of the guanidino-bearing hydantoin nor productive decarbamoylation to free arginine is established. This is a connected, conservation-compliant hypothesis, not a demonstrated route. Its uncertainty arises from these unresolved transformations, not from the number of steps or environments.', 'Hydration to ornithinamide is a conventional functional-group transformation, but it does not protect the alpha amino group. Consequently, rxn_014 still requires unproven delta-selective cyanamide guanidinylation in the presence of another primary amine. Controlled accumulation of the amide rather than further hydrolysis or competing reactions also needs an operating window. The route retains the upstream monohydrogenation and aminoaldehyde-cyclization problems. Argininamide hydrolysis could furnish the target chemically, but no conditions, yield or complete-sequence evidence establish that outcome here.', 'Completing carboxyl-group hydrolysis before guanidinylation avoids exposing a guanidino group to those hydrolysis operations. Nevertheless, the proposed scaffold synthesis and linear aminoaldehyde capture remain unsupported under specified conditions. The endpoint, rxn_017, requires efficient and selective guanidinylation of free ornithine by cyanamide. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrated guanidination of protein-bound ornithine using a laboratory reagent, not this free-monomer transformation with cyanamide. Thus, a plausible ornithine intermediate does not establish arginine production. Cyclization risks should be tested under the actual conditions rather than inferred universally from activated peptide chemistry.', 'Masking the alpha nitrogen in a hydantoin is a chemically meaningful response to competing amine guanidinylation and is more defensible than simply assuming delta selectivity in an unprotected diamine. However, formation and selective guanidinylation of the named ornithine hydantoin are not experimentally supported by the supplied literature. The masking strategy also creates a recovery requirement: rxn_022 and rxn_025 must release arginine without unacceptable side-chain modification. It does not address the earlier selective hydrogenation or aminoaldehyde-cyclization bottlenecks. All proposed transformations are conservation-compliant, but end-to-end target production remains unestablished.', 'N-alpha-carbamoylation plausibly reduces alpha-nitrogen nucleophilicity while leaving the terminal amine available for guanidinylation. This is a sound selectivity hypothesis, but neither its productive formation and persistence nor selective subsequent removal is demonstrated. The N-alpha-carbamoyl intermediate must be distinguished structurally from side-chain-carbamoylated isomers; formulas alone cannot do this. The decisive endpoint remains rxn_025, for which no selective arginine-releasing window is supplied. Earlier scaffold construction and unprotected aminoaldehyde processing remain additional chemical bottlenecks.', 'Hydantoin processing before guanidinylation avoids requiring a guanidino group to survive ring opening and decarbamoylation. However, the alpha-nitrogen mask has been removed before rxn_017, so this route does not obtain the selectivity advantage proposed for P7 and P8. Selective free-ornithine guanidinylation remains unresolved, while hydantoin formation and hydrolysis still require substrate-specific conditions and yields. The formal sequence is connected and balanced, but neither productive ornithine delivery through this scaffold route nor final arginine formation is established.', 'This is the best-supported candidate because the supplied discharge literature provides empirical evidence for an ornithine inventory, bypassing the unvalidated scaffold-construction hypothesis. That advantage is evidential, not a reward for having fewer steps. Nevertheless, rxn_026 is an aggregate source representation, not a demonstrated elementary reaction or measured product stoichiometry. Trace ornithine detection also does not establish an available concentration suitable for subsequent chemistry; collection and any analytical hydrolysis must be distinguished. The final cyanamide reaction remains unvalidated for selective production of free arginine. Longo et al. (2020) and the cited peptide work do not directly establish rxn_017.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine, stereochemistry unspecified\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This is a conditional experimental-hypothesis network, not an established prebiotic synthesis. The supplied literature does not establish production of free arginine from simple feedstocks and cannot substantiate ten independently demonstrated pathways. The ten pathways below are chemically distinct stage-of-guanidinylation variants of one scaffold-construction strategy; they are not ten independent confirmations. They compare installing the guanidino group before or after aldehyde formation, sulfur-assisted chain extension, Strecker chemistry, and hydrolysis. The scaffold is constructed from acetylene and three successive HCN-derived carbons, with cyanamide supplying the guanidino carbon. Unlike a lumped homologation edge, the proposed chain extension explicitly contains cyanohydrin formation, thioamide formation, photochemical deoxygenation, and nitrile reduction. Crucially, substrate-specific productive photodeoxygenation remains unverified: writing its mechanism and balance does not establish that it works. Every pathway therefore remains conditional, with additional selectivity and hydrolysis gaps identified individually. All numerical conditions below are proposed screening windows, not protocols extracted from the cited papers; the supplied report does not provide sufficient primary experimental detail to reproduce those protocols. No yields are invented. Reactions are staged in anoxic surface solutions, with transfer, reagent carryover, copper-sulfide precipitation, Fe(II) interference, and product survival requiring experimental validation rather than assumed purification. HCN, acetylene, ammonia, cyanamide, sulfide, and water are assumed locally available simple feedstocks; cyanamide availability has photochemical precedent in Lohrmann (1972). Neutral molecular formulas provide consistent reaction bookkeeping across changing protonation states. S8 represents a formal zero-valent-sulfur coproduct reservoir, not an experimentally established exclusive sulfur product. The schema has no coproduct role, so this terminal coproduct is marked intermediate. Stoichiometric coefficients and intended atom mappings are stated in mechanisms, never by repeating molecule IDs. In the intended arginine carbon mapping, the final HCN addition supplies the carboxyl carbon, the preceding HCN addition supplies the alpha carbon, the initial hydrocyanation supplies the beta carbon, acetylene supplies the gamma and delta carbons, and cyanamide supplies the guanidino carbon. No enantioselection is assumed.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inch