is proposed rather than the reported protocol. Ranjan et al. (2018) discuss environmental limitations on sulfide supply. Neither source establishes performance in the proposed iron-bearing, copper-sulfide-forming matrix."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cyanohydrin capture of beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: freshly generated aldehyde 0.1-5 mM, total cyanide 5-50 mM, pH 7-9, 10 min-24 h, initially shielded from UV. Measure reversible aldehyde release and condensation products."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_010 + mol_001 -> mol_011. Cyanide attacks the aldehyde carbonyl. The added HCN carbon becomes the new terminal nitrile carbon; the former aldehyde carbon becomes C2 bearing hydroxyl.", "reasoning": "Speculative for this particular unprotected substrate and operating window. Cyanohydrin chemistry is consistent with the reaction families discussed by Patel et al. (2015), but the supplied evidence does not demonstrate selective accumulation of this named intermediate. This step is explicitly separated from the subsequent, more uncertain deoxygenation."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrosulfide addition to the amino cyanohydrin nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, total sulfide 10-100 mM, pH 7-9, 1-72 h, dark. Identify thioamide constitution by NMR; test cyclic thioamide formation and cyanohydrin reversal."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_011 + mol_005 -> mol_012. Hydrosulfide addition and proton transfer convert the nitrile into a thioamide. The nitrile carbon and nitrogen become the thioamide carbon and nitrogen; sulfide supplies sulfur.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific extension of sulfur-mediated nitrile chemistry. Patel et al. (2015) provide relevant thioamide chemistry, but the supplied excerpts do not verify this exact intermediate or these conditions. The unprotected terminal amine creates additional cyclization possibilities, so a thioamide mass alone would not establish the proposed structure."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hypothesized photochemical deoxygenation of the amino hydroxythioamide", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Exploratory screen only: independently characterized thioamide 0.1-2 mM, pH 6-9, 254 nm irradiation at 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1, 0.5-24 h. Require carbon-, nitrogen-, oxygen-, and sulfur-product balances and dark controls."}, "mechanism": "Formal target stoichiometry: 8 mol_012 -> 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_006 + mol_007. The proposed transformation couples sulfur oxidation to reduction of the hydroxyl-bearing carbon, with dehydration restoring the nitrile. The thioamide carbon and nitrogen must become the product nitrile; the hydroxyl oxygen must leave in water. This is a mechanistic hypothesis, not an established elementary sequence.", "reasoning": "Decisive unverified scaffold-construction edge. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) account motivates examining thioamide-mediated chain extension but does not validate this specific photodeoxygenation. Atom balance and a proposed atom map are not evidence that the desired connectivity forms. If this reaction fails, every late-guanidinylation route using it fails."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Photoreduction of gamma-aminobutyronitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "Phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide complexes, proposed total Cu 10-100 micromolar", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: substrate 0.1-5 mM, total sulfide 5-50 mM, phosphate 10-50 mM, pH 6-8; 254 nm irradiation for 0.5-24 h. Sample rapidly to quantify the open-chain aldehyde versus cyclic imine inventory."}, "mechanism": "Formal stoichiometry: 8 mol_013 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_006 -> 8 mol_014 + 8 mol_003 + mol_007. Nitrile reduction and imine hydrolysis produce an aldehyde while releasing the nitrile nitrogen as ammonia. Subsequent five-membered cyclic imine formation is a competing reaction, not a productive ornithine-forming step.", "reasoning": "Speculative extrapolation from the shorter amino nitrile photoreduction in Patel et al. (2015). Gamma-aminobutanal can cyclize to a pyrroline, so a persistent linear aldehyde pool must not be assumed. This is an additional bottleneck specifically for routes that postpone guanidinylation beyond mol_013."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Cyanohydrin trapping of open-chain gamma-aminobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-305 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed competition experiment: transient aldehyde 0.1-2 mM, total cyanide 10-100 mM, pH 6.5-8.5, 1 min-6 h. Quantify open-chain cyanohydrin, cyclic imine, and cyclic cyanide-addition products separately."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_014 + mol_001 -> mol_015. Cyanide adds to the open-chain aldehyde, adding the fifth backbone carbon. Addition to a cyclic imine instead would produce a different skeleton and does not count as this edge.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific trapping step. General Strecker/cyanohydrin precedent does not establish competition against pyrroline formation. The cyclization issue emphasized in the supplied review must be resolved experimentally; detection of a formula-compatible nitrile is insufficient."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Open-chain Strecker conversion to ornithine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1 M, pH 7.5-9.5, 1-72 h. Determine linear versus cyclic aminonitrile structures and total recovery."}, "mechanism": "Intended net stoichiometry: mol_015 + mol_003 -> mol_016 + mol_006. Cyanohydrin reversibility permits aldehyde-imine formation and cyanide recapture; this is not direct displacement of hydroxide. Ammonia supplies the alpha-amino nitrogen.", "reasoning": "Speculative for selective open-chain ornithine-aminonitrile production. Patel et al. (2015) support relevant Strecker chemistry, and Johnson et al. (2008) and Parker et al. (2011) support an ornithine inventory in discharge samples, but those observations do not establish this particular mechanism or suppress cyclic products."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydration of ornithine aminonitrile to ornithinamide", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-353 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions; proposed pH 1.5-3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM, 1 h-14 days in a UV-shielded acidic pool analogue. Measure amide accumulation, further hydrolysis, and cyclization rather than assuming a selective stopping point."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_016 + mol_006 -> mol_017. Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration retains the nitrile carbon and nitrogen as the carboxamide carbon and nitrogen.", "reasoning": "Conventional reaction class, but a speculative operating window for this substrate in the supplied literature. No cited study establishes useful ornithinamide accumulation under these conditions. The purpose of this branch is to test whether nitrile processing can precede guanidinylation, not to claim demonstrated selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrolysis of ornithinamide to ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-370 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions; proposed pH 1-3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: amide 0.1-10 mM, 6 h-30 days, shielded from UV. Measure ornithine, residual amide, and cyclic products. Free ornithine accumulation is required before advancing to rxn_030."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_017 + mol_006 -> mol_018 + mol_003. Amide hydrolysis releases the original terminal nitrile nitrogen as ammonia and retains its carbon as the carboxyl carbon.", "reasoning": "Chemically conventional hydrolysis, but the exact substrate-specific prebiotic window is unverified. Johnson et al. (2008) and Parker et al. (2011) establish that ornithine can occur in discharge-derived inventories, not that this amide sequence generated it. Thoma and Powner (2023) concern activated peptide chemistry; their lactamization result is not treated as proof that free ornithine must disappear under every aqueous condition."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Photoreduction of guanidinylated beta-aminopropionitrile", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "Phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide complexes, proposed total Cu 10-100 micromolar", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: substrate 0.1-5 mM, total sulfide 5-50 mM, phosphate 10-50 mM, pH 6.5-8; 254 nm irradiation for 1-24 h. Measure guanidino-group retention, cyclic adduct formation, and sulfur speciation."}, "mechanism": "Formal stoichiometry: 8 mol_019 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_006 -> 8 mol_020 + 8 mol_003 + mol_007. Nitrile carbon becomes aldehyde carbon; nitrile nitrogen is released as ammonia. The guanidino carbon and all three guanidino nitrogens must remain associated with the side chain.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific continuation from the guanidinylated nitrile reported by Patel et al. (2015). The supplied evidence does not justify transferring the shorter free-amine photoreduction yield to this substrate or assuming a stable linear guanidino aldehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Cyanohydrin formation from the guanidino C3 aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: aldehyde 0.1-5 mM, total cyanide 5-50 mM, pH 6.5-8.5, 10 min-24 h. Quantify open-chain substrate availability and any intramolecular guanidino-aldehyde adducts."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_020 + mol_001 -> mol_021. HCN adds one backbone carbon as a nitrile; the former aldehyde carbon becomes the hydroxyl-bearing carbon.", "reasoning": "Speculative exact edge informed by the cyanohydrin chemistry in Patel et al. (2015). Guanidinylation removes the original primary amine but does not guarantee freedom from intramolecular aldehyde trapping by the guanidino group. Product identity and equilibrium abundance are required."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Formation of the guanidino hydroxythioamide", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, total sulfide 10-100 mM, pH 7-9, 1-72 h, dark. Determine whether nitrile-to-thioamide conversion outruns cyanohydrin dissociation and other sulfur chemistry."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_021 + mol_005 -> mol_022. Sulfide addition converts the nitrile carbon-nitrogen pair into a thioamide while preserving the guanidino substituent and carbon skeleton.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific step. Patel et al. (2015) motivate sulfur-mediated homologation chemistry but the supplied excerpts do not establish this isolated structure or its preparation. It is included as an explicit mechanistic test, not as a literature-validated missing link."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Hypothesized photochemical deoxygenation of the guanidino hydroxythioamide", "inputs": ["mol_022"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Exploratory screen only: independently characterized substrate 0.1-2 mM, pH 6-9, 254 nm irradiation at 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1, 0.5-24 h. Use dark controls and isotope-labelled carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen to establish the proposed connectivity."}, "mechanism": "Formal target stoichiometry: 8 mol_022 -> 8 mol_023 + 8 mol_006 + mol_007. Proposed sulfur oxidation accompanies reductive loss of the C2 hydroxyl group and restoration of the terminal nitrile. The thioamide carbon and nitrogen must become the nitrile; its sulfur enters the zero-valent-sulfur reservoir.", "reasoning": "Decisive unverified edge for the early-guanidinylation branches. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) report does not validate this explicit substrate-to-product conversion. No productive rate, yield, sulfur distribution, or guanidino-survival window is known from the supplied evidence. This cannot be promoted to an established synthesis merely because the equation balances."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Photoreduction to the guanidino C4 Strecker aldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_003", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "Phosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide complexes, proposed total Cu 10-100 micromolar", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: nitrile 0.1-5 mM, total sulfide 5-50 mM, phosphate 10-50 mM, pH 6.5-8; 254 nm irradiation for 1-24 h. Measure aldehyde selectivity, over-reduction, guanidino retention, and copper precipitation."}, "mechanism": "Formal stoichiometry: 8 mol_023 + 8 mol_005 + 8 mol_006 -> 8 mol_024 + 8 mol_003 + mol_007. Reduction and hydrolysis convert the nitrile carbon to the aldehyde carbon, which later becomes arginine's alpha carbon.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific photoreduction. Patel et al. (2015) support related precursor chemistry, whereas Cobb, Pudritz and Pearce (2015) use the arginine Strecker aldehyde computationally. The latter does not establish an experimental source, and the proposed transformation must not be cited as demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Formation of the guanidino C5 cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_025"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: aldehyde 0.1-5 mM, total cyanide 5-50 mM, pH 6.5-8.5, 10 min-24 h. Confirm the linear cyanohydrin and distinguish it from cyclic guanidino-aldehyde products."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_024 + mol_001 -> mol_025. The added HCN carbon becomes the nitrile carbon and ultimately arginine's carboxyl carbon. The aldehyde carbon becomes the future alpha carbon.", "reasoning": "Chemically motivated precursor step associated with the cyanohydrin/Strecker endgame discussed by Patel et al. (2015), but the numerical conditions and accumulation of this exact linear species are unverified here. It does not remedy the upstream aldehyde-supply gap."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Strecker formation of arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_025", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_026", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1 M, pH 7.5-9.5, 1-72 h. Quantify arginine aminonitrile with an authentic standard and assess mixed-feedstock compatibility."}, "mechanism": "Intended net stoichiometry: mol_025 + mol_003 -> mol_026 + mol_006. Reversible cyanohydrin opening, imine formation, and cyanide addition replace the alpha hydroxyl function with an ammonia-derived amino group.", "reasoning": "Strongest downstream precursor-level support: Patel et al. (2015) report an arginine alpha-aminonitrile-forming step, with 45-to-46 tabulated at 90% in the supplied account. That isolated conversion is not an overall network yield, does not validate the proposed screening conditions, and does not demonstrate free arginine or integrated one-pot production."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Hydration of arginine aminonitrile to argininamide", "inputs": ["mol_026", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_027"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-353 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions; proposed pH 1.5-3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM, 1 h-14 days, shielded from UV. Follow nitrile disappearance, argininamide, arginine, and guanidino-degradation products with time-resolved analysis."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_026 + mol_006 -> mol_027. Acid-catalyzed nitrile hydration retains the nitrile carbon and nitrogen in the carboxamide.", "reasoning": "Unverified arginine-specific endpoint processing. Patel et al. (2015) do not report hydrolysis through to free arginine. Nitrile hydration is chemically conventional, so it is not rejected intrinsically; however, a useful rate and guanidino-preserving window have not been established by the supplied research."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Hydrolytic release of free arginine from argininamide", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_028", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-370 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions; proposed pH 1-3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: argininamide 0.1-10 mM, 6 h-30 days, UV-shielded. Require a time window with net free-arginine accumulation. Run authentic arginine stability controls at every pH and temperature; quantify citrulline and ornithine alongside arginine."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_027 + mol_006 -> mol_028 + mol_003. Carboxamide hydrolysis releases its nitrogen as ammonia while preserving the alpha-amino and side-chain guanidino groups.", "reasoning": "Unverified target-forming step under the proposed environmental conditions. The supplied literature contains no demonstrated completion of the Patel et al. (2015) precursor route to free arginine. Guanidino degradation must be measured rather than presumed inevitable; neither generic hydrolysis plausibility nor potential degradation establishes the outcome. Authentic-standard chromatography plus orthogonal structural confirmation is required."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Early guanidinylation of beta-aminopropionitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-353 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: amino nitrile 1-10 mM, cyanamide 10-100 mM, pH 8-11, 1-30 days. Determine conversion, cyanamide dimerization, and nitrile retention. No synthetic scandium catalyst is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_009 + mol_004 -> mol_019. The sole primary amine attacks the cyanamide carbon, followed by proton transfers to a substituted guanidine. Cyanamide contributes one carbon and two nitrogens.", "reasoning": "Direct precursor-level support: Patel et al. (2015) report this guanidinylation as relatively inefficient. It has a genuine selectivity advantage because no alpha-amino group yet competes. The proposed screen is not the reported protocol, and neither high conversion nor compatibility with subsequent photochemistry is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Hypothesized productive guanidinylation channel at the C3 aminoaldehyde stage", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_020"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-323 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Exploratory competition screen: freshly generated aminoaldehyde 0.1-2 mM, cyanamide 1-50 mM, pH 6-10, 1 min-72 h. Quantify linear guanidino aldehyde versus cyclic condensation products. Do not assign the reported adduct 37 to mol_020 without structural proof."}, "mechanism": "Intended productive-channel stoichiometry: mol_010 + mol_004 -> mol_020. Addition of the terminal amine to cyanamide would retain the aldehyde. Competing aldehyde-cyanamide condensation can instead create cyclic species and is not represented as successful passage through this edge.", "reasoning": "Speculative and likely strongly competitive. Patel et al. (2015) report efficient formation of an aminoaldehyde/cyanamide adduct, not proof that a useful pool of the linear aldehyde drawn here accumulates. This variant tests whether productive open-chain material is accessible; it must be rejected if the observed chemistry is only the competing adduct."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Guanidinylation at the C4 cyanohydrin stage", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, cyanamide 5-100 mM, pH 8-10, 1 h-14 days. Measure cyanohydrin dissociation and aldehyde-cyanamide trapping alongside guanidinylation."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_011 + mol_004 -> mol_021. The terminal primary amine adds to cyanamide while the hydroxynitrile functionality is retained.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific reaction, motivated by the amine guanidinylation precedent in Patel et al. (2015). Temporary carbonyl masking as a cyanohydrin gives this timing variant a chemical rationale, but that mask is reversible and does not establish protection from competing aldehyde chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_024", "name": "Guanidinylation at the hydroxythioamide stage", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Exploratory screen: characterized thioamide 0.1-5 mM, cyanamide 5-100 mM, pH 8-10, 1 h-14 days, dark. Identify whether reaction occurs at the terminal amine, thioamide nitrogen, or sulfur."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_012 + mol_004 -> mol_022. Productive terminal-amine addition to cyanamide preserves the hydroxythioamide required by rxn_015. Alternative sulfur- or thioamide-centred reactions are not counted as this product.", "reasoning": "Speculative with no direct supporting example in the supplied research. Its rationale is that the thioamide nitrogen is resonance-deactivated relative to an aliphatic amine, but sulfur introduces competing nucleophilicity. Patel et al. (2015) support only the broader amine/cyanamide concept, not positional selectivity for this substrate."}, {"id": "rxn_025", "name": "Guanidinylation of gamma-aminobutyronitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_023"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-353 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: amino nitrile 1-10 mM, cyanamide 10-100 mM, pH 8-11, 1-30 days. Establish conversion before exposing the substrate to aldehyde-generating photoreduction."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_013 + mol_004 -> mol_023. The sole primary amine adds to cyanamide; the four-carbon nitrile skeleton remains unchanged.", "reasoning": "Speculative homologous-substrate extension of the guanidinylation demonstrated by Patel et al. (2015). Installing guanidine here could avoid the particularly problematic free gamma-aminoaldehyde stage without exposing guanidine to the preceding deoxygenation. This is a meaningful timing distinction, not evidence that the upstream scaffold or this guanidinylation is established."}, {"id": "rxn_026", "name": "Hypothesized guanidinylation of transient gamma-aminobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Exploratory kinetic competition screen: transient aminoaldehyde 0.1-2 mM, cyanamide 10-100 mM, pH 6-10, 1 min-24 h. Quantify cyclic imine formation and require direct evidence for the linear guanidino aldehyde."}, "mechanism": "Intended productive-channel stoichiometry: mol_014 + mol_004 -> mol_024. Terminal-amine addition to cyanamide must occur before, or draw material from, competing intramolecular imine formation.", "reasoning": "Highly speculative. The supplied research identifies aminoaldehyde cyclization and aldehyde-cyanamide trapping as serious problems; Patel et al. (2015) do not establish selective formation of this linear product. Slow cyanamide guanidinylation may lose the kinetic competition entirely. This pathway is retained only as an explicitly falsifiable ordering hypothesis."}, {"id": "rxn_027", "name": "Guanidinylation of the C5 amino cyanohydrin", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_025"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-333 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: cyanohydrin 0.1-5 mM, cyanamide 5-100 mM, pH 8-10, 1 h-14 days. Measure free-aldehyde release, cyclic imine formation, and guanidinylated-product connectivity."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_015 + mol_004 -> mol_025. The terminal amine adds to cyanamide before installation of the alpha-amino group.", "reasoning": "Speculative, with general amine/cyanamide support from Patel et al. (2015). This ordering avoids alpha-versus-delta amine discrimination, but a cyanohydrin is not an irreversible protecting group. The aldehyde and cyclization problems therefore remain experimentally relevant."}, {"id": "rxn_028", "name": "Attempted delta-selective guanidinylation of ornithine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-353 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: diamino nitrile 0.1-10 mM, cyanamide 1-100 mM, pH 8-11, 1 h-30 days. Quantify alpha-, delta-, and bis-guanidinylated products using authentic standards and NMR; mass spectrometry alone cannot assign the guanidinylation site."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_016 + mol_004 -> mol_026. Only addition through the terminal side-chain amine yields arginine aminonitrile. Alpha-amino attack gives a constitutional isomer.", "reasoning": "Speculative positional selectivity. Patel et al. (2015) demonstrate guanidinylation of a substrate with only one primary amine, not this diamine. The electron-withdrawing nitrile changes alpha-amine basicity and nucleophilicity, but that observation does not establish delta selectivity at any proposed pH."}, {"id": "rxn_029", "name": "Attempted delta-selective guanidinylation of ornithinamide", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_027"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-353 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: ornithinamide 0.1-10 mM, cyanamide 1-100 mM, pH 8-11, 1 h-30 days. Resolve alpha-, delta-, and bis-guanidinylation, carboxamide hydrolysis, and cyclic products."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_017 + mol_004 -> mol_027. Productive guanidinylation occurs at the side-chain amine. Conversion of the nitrile into an amide does not protect the alpha-amino group.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate-specific selectivity. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrate guanidination of ornithine residues in a synthetic protein using a laboratory reagent, not cyanamide conversion of free ornithinamide. This ordering reduces guanidino exposure to nitrile hydration but retains the competing-primary-amine problem."}, {"id": "rxn_030", "name": "Attempted delta-selective cyanamide guanidinylation of free ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_028"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-353 K", "pressure": "1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: ornithine 0.1-10 mM, cyanamide 1-100 mM, pH 8-11, 1 h-30 days. Require free-arginine accumulation and quantify alpha-guanidinoornithine, bis-guanidinylation, cyanamide dimerization, and residual ornithine. pH is not assumed to confer delta selectivity."}, "mechanism": "Intended stoichiometry: mol_018 + mol_004 -> mol_028. The side-chain primary amine adds to cyanamide, supplying the missing guanidino carbon and two nitrogens without requiring subsequent carboxyl-group hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Unverified target-forming step under prebiotic conditions. Longo et al. (2020) establish protein-side-chain guanidination with a laboratory reagent, not efficient delta-selective cyanamide guanidinylation of free orn